## Ch21 — The Landing

1. **Lira (cutaway), the three carters.** Way in: the second carter's question back to her ("You buying a wagon or selling one?") — then fold back to how she set it up. Her rule from the floor: never go to the same corner three times; a carter remembers a girl who asks the same thing twice. Three errands that are real (Rooke's crate of frame springs off the river barge; the Stone's letters to the river post; a parcel of tape). The grey coats at the landing look past her — she's the program's Wind with tape on her wrists, not the subject; she prices that and feels insulted and glad.
   - Carter one (day 58): old, mule team, freight. Subject: manifests. Every chartered station stamps a manifest; past the last one nobody stamps anything, "and you don't know you've crossed till you look for the stamp and there isn't one". He goes past.
   - Carter two (day 61): a woman with a covered wagon, wool west and salt back. Subject: days and fords. Four days to the last station loaded, three if light; the fords; the woman's verdict on what's past it: nobody asks your tier because there's nobody with a book. She goes past.
   - Carter three (day 64): young, quiet, a new wagon. Subject: the Line itself — where it is. He tells her exactly where the last station stands. He turns at it. Asked why, he looks at his horse's ears and says he turns there; won't say more. She doesn't push; she enters it.
   - Lira on the bluff road that evening adding it up alone; what she wants (her own feet out of a room) beside what she has (Rooke's card, the Stone, a ladder). She writes the note on the board in the morning — numbers not names.
2. **Cael, day 66.** He reads the board at the covered walk; knows the five is Seln. At the wing's counter Seln, filing, gives him the inventory in his ferryman economy: the five drawers are full and shut. Cael doesn't ask who told him.
3. **Day 68, the small room.** Way in: Brom coming down the residence passage with the common room's one good chair on his shoulder (humor: "It's for the council." He sits in it). The room: an old farm store under the residence — one door, a shuttered slit on a wall six feet off, a drain in the corner, a table that came in before the door was narrowed; voices die in it. Seating: Seln on the bench's middle with nothing in front of him; Karis's papers face-down at the far end; one lamp. Cael counts.
4. Lira asks: why are we still answering? [P-sense; own words] Then the carters as a sum (brief, since we've seen them): the Line is where the last station stands; manifests stop being stamped; two go past; the third won't.
5. Karis answers standing. Way in: she lays one copied sheet face up — a default ruling, two paragraphs — and reads the second paragraph first: the respondent's own letter quoted as evidence. Then who he was: a coastal guild practitioner, an integrity matter, winning, who wrote a good letter saying he'd lost faith in the process, and stopped attending; the registry thanked him and ruled by default, citing him. Then the registry's word: *fled*. Then her list of times text held (in reverse: Norhold's clause, Halcenvane's true book, Greyvane's hearing, Vell's ledger at Ardenmere, Denvash where there was no text). Her price: leaving is a move the machine has a form for. Seln's one correction, eyes shut: forty days after the fourth sitting closes, not the next sitting; the code counts days. Karis corrects the notebook.

## Ch22 — Both Roads

1. Brom's three sentences [P "Prepare both roads"], counted by Lira. The word *box* stays on the table. Lira asks what's in his. Shirts; all the strapping; the plaque, because Lira would go back for it. Then [P] "It's the second box that's the problem." Hand to the LEFT pocket. Nobody asks.
2. Cael argues the first pole at full strength. Way in: Karis says "You're holding one back" before he's finished, and he starts again from the bottom: Denvash — a woman with a brass plate, eleven seconds, no text (Pellin as a woman per Book 1); then forward: Vell's ledger, the Greyvane hearing and the clause, the grant and two renewals on paper, an inspection that found nothing, a tournament their own clause put him in, an instrument whose keeper wrote that no number goes there. People change with weather; text doesn't; it's ground. [P] "I'm not leaving the room while the text still works." Lira: the seal said lawful and proceeding. He: lawful is forever; every corridor had a door nobody read. Brom: and if this code has none? Cael: then it's the first.
3. Karis names it: an induction — every case pointing one way is the shape of a law until the next case. She'd file it tomorrow. No count makes it a law.
4. Lira: her Fenmark form — correct, stamped, filed by somebody kind, and it opened under her anyway. [P] "It's not ground. It's a floor." Floors stand on something; she can't see what. Check what it stands on. *Both roads.* Cael agrees to that and concedes nothing; the room hears it.
5. Seln's silence, watched by Cael: his looks (Lira at "east"; Cael long at "the text still works"). Cael has no word for the look.
6. Tea. Brom makes it; twenty minutes; nobody leaves — that's the finding. Lira works her landing-leg hip. Karis: on the record, we decided nothing. Lira [P] "It was the night we asked." Karis writes it. Cael's log line about Seln: a bill, not an argument; a question mark.
7. **Seln (cutaway), the dark wing.** Way in: the side door key that sticks; no lamp; the fire-watch lantern under the north arch puts a square on the counter. He sits at the copying table under the shelf where the case is. Counts the council the way he counts anything: what was said, what he said (a number put right because a wrong number in a room he sits in is his). Then his trade, counted: fifteen years, nineteen postings (kinds, not names), sixty-three subjects; he followed some past their files and stopped. The maintenance records: twelve years ago, a lawful room, another errand, copied by habit; months later, in a corridor between two ordinary appointments, understood. Never what. *Whose question it was.* The boy: inferred — the mill-town table and *too tidy*; two slips, each burned on the coals without a word; a season of figures that never settled; Norhold spent on purpose. The boy arguing with a malfunction. The key in his waistcoat; he takes it out, holds it, puts it back; does not take the case down. [P] *the deciding has stopped being quiet.*

## Ch23 — Colors

1. Way in: day 69 dawn, the coach yard — the colors trunk open for the first time since Norhold; Bracken reading the travel sheet. Back one step to day 67: Bracken asked, as his office makes him, whether a named respondent's colors should travel; Withrow's one sentence.
2. The road north (two days; a waystation; Karis drafting on her knee; Seln with the case left behind on its shelf — he travels light). The confluence at dusk: the tongue of land, two rivers, the hall at the point. The inn: the framed ruling still behind the counter; the innkeeper reads it to them again.
3. The card: one line — the lighter, earlier card of a non-qualifying year. The steward's board: Rooke's filing under the host's exhibition provision, a host option he exercised for his program; *five exchanges at most, two touches ends it*. The Silver Rank Eight's line: Force, the confluence's bargemen's guild, Silver for years; he accepted inside an hour.
4. Rooke's brief, four minutes, at the inn table with bread and salt for courses. Courses under courses; the anchoring four-count. Lose the first slowly and cheaply (one burst). The second is the program's exam. Third and fourth are his. The Stone adds one line (his knees, not his hands). Lira's "growth" joke.
5. Dawn, day 71: Rooke walks the hall alone pressing planks; Cael comes in; three facts charted — no light (lamps high on the beam, river doors shut), no rebound (ropes new-tensioned), a quiet side that pulls (the panel's table breaks the east rope; no benches there). A fourth line left empty. The repair under the south-west corner (after the flood came under the river doors) is not seen — sawdust and the rope's shadow.
6. Warm beat: Lira can't eat; Brom makes her; "honesty".

## Ch24 — Two Touches to One

The bout. Exhibition, five exchanges at most, two touches ends it; touches land E1 (his), E2 (hers), E4 (his); E3 clean. Lira's bursts: E1 one, E2 two, E3 two (the floor's free five spent), E4 the sixth — past the day's five on planks — which comes half a stride short.
1. Way in: the R8 at his mark, not moving; the steward's call.
2. E1: he doesn't come forward; he builds where he stands and the floor arrives at her. Her one measuring burst at his structure's base finds a second course under the first. He's taken the centre; he builds her toward the south-west; her back foot twice on boards that answer differently. A course finishes; the stamp pushes the floor at her; she goes away into the north-west post; the rope gives nothing. While she's on it, his palm. "Touch. The confluence." She's up before the steward. Rooke writes one word.
3. E2: the program's language — the four-count drill; she goes at his building hand in the first breath, before the course has a count; he must finish or cover; he covers; his second course never sets. She's inside, at contact. The quiet east side used on purpose for the re-entry. Touch at the ribs off her third burst. "Touch. Halcenvane." He steps back a full stride; changes — fights the woman, not the card.
4. E3: his actual depth; courses faster than her intervals; he gives up the centre and she reads it half a beat late as a gift; the false quarter under her push foot; three-quarter purchase. She gets out of a finished structure at contact range (bursts four and five), out by the south rope, no touch. Cael enters the fourth fact now.
5. E4: he fights at range and refuses to be interesting. She goes after it twice; the second time the sixth burst comes short by half a stride; the touch lands while she's still arriving. "Touch. The confluence. Two to one." The figures (his 28, hers 27). Handshake held a beat long; a word each.
6. The Stone on his feet at the quiet end, applauding over his head where the river academy's people can see him.
7. At the rope: Lira's lesson first, in her words; Rooke: "Yes." Then Rooke writes [P] "Again. Faster. It's there." aloud. The bill: six bursts against three; one past the floor's five; a bruise across the shoulder blades from the post; the landing leg hot; and the line about the south-west boards — *I walked this floor at dawn. Mine.*

## Ch25 — The Rail

1. Way in: the main door's draught. The officer in registry grey with a case held in front of him in both hands, walking the inside of the north benches during the Current third-year's bout. Cael times it: fifty-one seconds; by the twentieth the hall is watching him, not the floor. He does not send his clerk.
2. Service on the delegation: a production order — the house's evaluation-linked floor records for the enrollee, year to date, to be produced at the fourth sitting. Bracken stands and reads it whole, both sides; signs; asks for the second copy. Withrow thanks the officer by his grade, pitched for four rows.
3. The hall adjusting, counted: the north benches that stood for Lira now applaud the Current girl seated; a steward stops finding chairs; two correspondents follow the officer out; the innkeeper at the door doesn't look at them. Lawful; could have been posted; the timing was the message.
4. Lira at the rope with Cael (new exchange, own words): they waited until she'd lost well.
5. **Lira (cutaway), day 72 evening, the emptying hall.** The bout sheet: touches, figures, Rooke's four words, the bill. The ladder counted rung by rung: Iron Rank One, the program figure, a Silver who'd fought her as a Silver, the Stone's hands, the advancement evaluation, *Silver bracket. Next cycle.* What she will not say yet: four nights ago she asked the leaving question and meant it, and tonight she holds the best page she's ever been handed, and the road east has no Silver card on it. Cael at the rail with the order's copy (Bracken gave it to him to carry for Karis). [P] "One of these is the best night of my life." / "Noted." She folds the sheet along its creases, slowly; doesn't say the other half.
6. Short Cael beat on the road home: a log line.

## Ch26 — One Question

1. Way in: the assistant, weeks ago, finding a dead heading in the code's index while learning to "find it" — *Of the seat's hearing, and who may be heard* — and bringing it to Karis, who traced it: the respondent's advocate of the record may put a question to the petitioner through counsel, for the minute; and (second paragraph) may be heard at the close by the seat's leave. Last used about a hundred and forty years ago. The counsel's rule: one question, once. "Leave the second paragraph where it is. Till the end." (light setup for M8)
2. Day 75: the side door; the young officer enters Karis not as *documentary support* but, at the counsel's written request, as *advocate of the record*; he has to look up how to spell it.
3. The fourth sitting: Bracken's floor book produced under the order and receipted; Havel at the junior chair; Ilsev in the evaluation seat. The counsel rises under the old provision; the hood permits; she reads Karis's card [P] *What evidence, if any, would defeat the petition?* The wave down the benches; the clerk's pen stops; Ilsev looks up and keeps looking. Jent rises without consulting anyone, doesn't ask to answer in writing; [P] "None, Advocate. The petition doesn't turn on evidence. It turns on discretion." Sits.
4. Cael: the sentence everyone in the house has drawn as a corridor said out loud by the man sent to walk them down it, because he was asked.
5. Karis writes it in her front matter. On the steps: the counsel to Karis about the price; Karis on what frightens her — he answered because the answer was obvious to him.
6. Evening: the house's counsel up the hill with them to work the produced record; stays in the guest room.
7. **Day 76, the gate.** Way in: the lodge-man doing a thing he has never done (shutting the lodge window on the road and coming out without his hat); the boy runs. Cael from the wall stair to the gatehouse gallery above the arch — a stretch of walk with a waist-high parapet behind the bell-frame. The court's geometry (exits: the arch, the lodge door, the covered walk's arch, the lane round the lecture range, the stable passage — the only one with no door). Four riders, no livery, good horses.
8. Withrow arrives with the counsel; follows the lodge's posted board (registry instruments: the registrar, the hour noted). She requires it read aloud. The order [P, verbatim body] with a six-word heading naming him (*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward.*); eighty-one words, Cael counts. Bracken's minute and forty seconds on the seal; genuine stock, registry format, issuing office unknown to him in thirteen years of this counter; "a facilities office, newly constituted"; constitution instrument; effective on presentation by an issuing authority.
   Interleaved: pass one (dismount, she stops three steps on — nothing), pass two (during Bracken: she's on the court's one level flag, over the capped well).

## Ch27 — Measure the Door

1. Continue the gate: Withrow's four-sentence refusal; pass three (the arch's whole span from her angle); "voluntarily" — offered up, chin lifted a degree, toward the empty-looking gallery; standing still costs Cael in his hands; Withrow does not look up; her answer (the institution; in writing; to her). Pass four (every exit priced; she's squared to the stable passage; nothing laid; the remount without the half-second). No written refusal asked for; they leave at a working trot. Withrow: log the hour, the reading, the offer, that it went up to an empty gallery. *Measure the door.*
2. Noon refectory: the rumor in three versions; the open seat at the third-years' table by the west windows; Rooke sits in it and eats a whole meal and talks about hips; Ephram next; then the rest.
3. Gault on the covered walk [P] "I have written that down." It steadies Cael more than anything.
4. The notebook: the first Anchor page (own words; hypothesis; two tests; a shape and no numbers; the gap marked) [P] *Query for Seln: what Path holds people?*
5. Records hall, evening, Bracken's copy on the long table. Seln: Anchor Path; rare; nobody's champion; the service takes them early; enforcement; [P] "Whoever wrote that paper is done assessing." Karis draws the distinction once (Ternhall's anchors — planted footholds that drink heat; the Lattice Path — lines you can't see; this — a Path that holds people). Seln on her grade. Karis with Bracken's glass on the seal; the query to the district seat will come back paper all the way down; [P] the faceless layer has learned to write paper; the *yet*. Lira: what does it do when the paper doesn't work? No answer.
6. The Crown yard after dark: Brom: "Show me." Cael, Brom and Seln (Seln comes unasked). Porter's chalk on the oak; the court drawn at size; the exits; Brom stands on her flag; Seln walks each line out and back; the long wall throws their voices back so they talk low. They learn where they'd have to go and that every way crosses her; they don't learn how far she reaches. Seln: leave the blank where you can see it.
7. Back in the hall: the copy where they left it; Seln sits and lays his hands flat on either side of it and looks longer than anyone; he has stopped turning his cup. Close [P] "Whatever we're going to do, the schedule isn't ours anymore."

