     3	The Silver program's first road meet fell at the term's midpoint, at the river academy's own hall two days north — the confluence town, the season's best floor, the place a regional Iron title had been decided a year before — and the delegation went to it as a delegation for the first time since Norhold, because Withrow had ruled that Halcenvane's Silver entrant traveled under the academy's colors with the academy's people, proceedings or no proceedings, and the ruling had the whole of her tenure in it.
     4	
     5	She had made it at the delegation table, in one sentence, in front of Bracken, who had raised the question because raising questions was his office: whether the academy's colors ought to travel to a public meet in a term when the academy was a named respondent.
     6	
     7	"The academy has an entrant in a Silver bracket for the first time in sixteen years," Withrow said. "We will be at the rail in our own colors, and if that is inconvenient to anyone at Ostrand they may put it in a filing, and I will read it."
     8	
     9	Cael went because Lira's road had been his to chart since a boarding-house table in Ardenmere, and because a bout at Silver was a thing he intended to have catalogued from its first flag. Brom went because Brom went. Karis went with the response's fourth-session draft in a document wallet, because the proceedings did not pause for a meet and she did not pause for anything. Seln went as records support and took, at the confluence hall, the gallery seat where the sightlines to the floor and the doors converged. Rooke coached. The Silver Rank 5 from the river academy — her instructor, her assessor, her first Silver — came home to his own hall to watch his student fight up a tier on the floor where he had lost to her at Iron.
    10	
    11	The Silver bracket at a regional meet was four fighters, and her draw was the deepest of them: a Silver Rank 8, Force Path, a career practitioner of the confluence's own guild who had held the region's Silver standing for six years and had, the file said, never once fought an Iron in a rated bout, because Irons did not enter Silver brackets. The file had a line for the enrollee's tier. It read *Iron Rank 1, Silver program, Halcenvane — entered under the program provision*. The guild veteran had read it, and had said, at the draw, with a professional's plainness that the delegation extracted from a steward, "Six years at Silver and I finally get a bout somebody will watch."
    12	
    13	Rooke's pre-bout brief ran to four minutes at the residence table the night before, and Cael wrote all of it down.
    14	
