    27	The floor was the confluence hall's: new boards, true and fast, a full rope square with gallery on all four sides and the judges' dais breaking the east rail, the floor where she had taken a Force builder apart a year ago by taxing his structures at their unfinished edges. She knew its every property. So did he. He had trained on it for six years.
    28	
    29	Cael charted the geometry from the north gallery before the flags, because a floor was a document and this one had four things in it worth entering.
    30	
    31	The square was thirty-two feet, roped at three heights, and the ropes had been re-tensioned that autumn and had almost no give, which meant the boundary was a wall rather than a cushion and a fighter driven onto it did not get a rebound. The boards were new and fast and had been laid the previous summer over the old floor, and they ran north to south, and at the square's south-west quarter the run changed direction where a repair had gone in — eight feet of boards laid crossways, level with the rest, visually identical from the gallery, different underfoot. The dais broke the east rail, which meant the east side had no gallery pressure and no noise, and fighters drifted toward silence without knowing it; Cael had watched that happen in three of the afternoon's Iron bouts and had timed the drift at about nine seconds. And the hall's high lamps were rigged on the crane-line above the square's centre and threw nothing usefully directional, which meant that unlike a mill town's loading doors, this floor had no light to spend.
