    85	That was the word the log used, and the log did not use words like that, and it used it because there was no other: she came to the rail with her collar dark and her hands taped and a light on her that Cael had seen once before, at a registry station's anteroom, and had not seen since — because the loss was the *right kind*. The kind with six more years of doors in it. And for the first time in her life, every door was open, and someone was holding each one, and the someone had an institution's seal on his file.
    86	
    87	Rooke's post-bout note went into the coaching file at the rail, four words, Rooke-length, and he read them aloud as he wrote them, which he did not do:
    88	
    89	"Again. Faster. It's there."
    90	
    91	The bill came out with the file, because Rooke invoiced everything. Seven bursts across four exchanges, three of them over rate. The strapped shoulder, which had been a heat-wrap matter since Norhold and was now a five-days-at-half-work matter. A boundary contact at the north-west post in the first exchange, which would be a bruise the width of a hand by the following night and was already stiffening. And one line that was not an injury and went in the file anyway, because Rooke had never once separated craft from cost: *three-quarter purchase, SW quarter, ex. 3. She didn't know the boards changed. Nobody told her. That's mine.*
    92	
    93	The lesson was one sentence and she said it herself, at the rail, before Rooke could, which he noted too.
    94	
    95	"It isn't the craft. The craft got in twice. It's the rate — I was half a stride short at the end and the half-stride was the shoulder." She flexed the hand. "I don't need a different bout. I need to be faster inside the same one."
    96	
    97	"Yes," said Rooke.
    98	
    99	She slept that night with the bout sheet on the residence table where she could see it from the bed and the shoulder strapped for the five days Rooke had priced, and Cael knew this because she told him so at breakfast, unprompted, with the sheet in her hand, and did not seem embarrassed, which was itself the year's whole distance.
   100	
   101	---
   102	
   103	The counterweight came braided through the meet in two movements, and the first was timed for the gallery.
   104	
   105	Mid-meet, between the Silver bracket's second and third bouts, with the confluence hall at its fullest and Halcenvane's colors on the floor for a second bout — a third-year in the Iron bracket, the program's own supporting entrant — a registry records officer came through the hall's main door with a document case and a clerk.
   106	
   107	He did not send the clerk. That was the first thing Cael charted, from the north gallery, with the whole hall laid out beneath him like a floor before a bout. The officer came in at the main door, which was at the hall's south end and was the door the paying gallery used, and he walked the length of the hall on the gallery's inside rail, past three hundred seated people, in registry grey, with a document case held at the front of his body in both hands where it could be seen — and it took him, Cael timed it, fifty-one seconds to reach the delegation's block, and by the twentieth second the bout on the floor had stopped being what the hall was watching.
   108	
   109	He served Halcenvane's delegation, in front of the whole confluence, with a documentary demand from the proceedings: the respondent institution's complete evaluation-linked floor records for the enrollee, term to date, to be produced at the fourth session.
   110	
   111	Bracken took it. Bracken was the officer of the academy present who was entitled to take it, and he stood to do so, and he read the whole instrument standing, front and back, at reading pace, which took ninety seconds during which nobody in the delegation's block sat down either. Then he signed the acknowledgment, and asked the officer for the second copy, and received it, and thanked him, and sat.
   112	
   113	Withrow had not moved. She thanked the officer as he went past her, by rank, in a voice pitched for the four rows around her, and the four rows heard a chancellor thank a registry officer for the correct service of a lawful instrument, and did not have anywhere to put it.
   114	
   115	The demand was lawful. Bracken would confirm it by letter within the week — a routine production order, the code's ordinary instrument for building a record, served on a respondent's delegation because the delegation was where the respondent's officers were. It could have been served at the bluff. It could have been posted. It was served at a venue, at the meet's fullest hour, at the gallery rail, by an officer who walked the whole length of the hall to do it, and the timing was the message, and every registry-literate person in the confluence read the message as it was delivered.
   116	
   117	Lira watched it from the floor's edge, taping for the third bout, and Cael, at the rail, charted what she watched: her academy's colors getting quieter in the hall, in real time — the confluence's regard for Halcenvane's Silver entrant, sixty seconds earlier the warmest thing in the building, adjusting itself around the sight of a registry officer serving the delegation paper. Not hostility. *Caution.* The hall recalculating what it cost to be seen cheering for the respondent's people.
   118	
   119	He could count it. That was the part he wrote down and disliked writing. The east gallery's second and third rows had come to their feet for her second exchange; at the third bout's call they applauded the Halcenvane third-year, seated, at half length, in the manner of a room being correct. A steward who had spent the morning finding the delegation chairs found them nothing in the afternoon. Two of the four correspondents' boards left the hall behind the officer.
   120	
   121	"They served it *here*," she said, at the rail, afterward, in the voice that had once discovered an institution was small and now discovered one was patient. "Not at the bluff. Not at Ostrand. Here, in the middle of my bracket, in front of the river."
   122	
   123	"It's a production order," Cael said. "It's lawful."
   124	
   125	"I know what it is." She finished the tape. "I know what it was *for*, too. So does everyone in this hall. They just don't have Karis's word for it."
   126	
   127	"Corridor," Cael said.
   128	
   129	"That's the one." She bit the tape off. "It's a good word. I'd like a better one by spring."
   130	
   131	---
   132	
   133	The second movement was Karis's, and it happened at Ostrand the same week, at the fourth session, and it closed the term's legal question for good.
   134	
   135	She was not counsel. She could not argue. But the code permitted the respondent's advocate to put questions to the petitioner's counsel on the record in an institutional matter, through the counsel, for the minute — a fossil provision from the era when proceedings were conversations rather than schedules — and Karis had found it in the third week of term and had been waiting four sessions for the question worth spending it on.
   136	
   137	She had found it, she told the table afterward, in the code's own index, under a heading that had been left in place when the practice under it had died: *interrogatories, of counsel, at the seat*. Nine words of enabling text and a hundred and forty years since the last recorded use, which she had traced. The counsel had read the nine words twice and said that she would put one question and not two, because a provision nobody has invoked in a century survives being used once and does not survive being used twice, and Karis had agreed, and had then spent four sessions declining to spend it — through the empanelment, through the preliminary ruling, through the exhibits session and two scheduling sittings — because the whole value of one question is what you do not ask with it.
   138	
   139	The counsel put it, at the fourth session, in the exact words Karis had written on a card: *What evidence, if any, would defeat the petition?*
   140	
   141	The room understood the question before Jent answered it. Cael, at the respondent's table, watched forty benches of registry-literate people arrive at it in a wave that reached the back of the hall about two seconds after it reached the front, and watched the clerk's pen stop, and watched Ilsev, in the evaluation seat, look up from her file for the first time that session and keep looking up.
   142	
   143	Jent rose. He did not consult his clerk. He did not ask the seat for leave to take the question in writing, which he was entitled to do and which the counsel had told the table he would probably do.
   144	
   145	He answered it courteously, precisely, without a pause, in the voice of a man stating the weather, and the clerk entered it in the minute, and the minute was a public record, and the sentence entered the registry's archive forever:
   146	
   147	"None, Advocate. The petition doesn't turn on evidence. It turns on discretion."
   148	
   149	He sat down.
   150	
   151	That was the whole of it. No qualification, no softening, no procedural cushion of the kind available to him in six different forms — a man asked a direct question about the instrument he was holding, answering it accurately, at the correct height, because lying to a seat was not something he did and because the true answer cost him nothing at all.
   152	
   153	The winning-doesn't-matter sentence — the one Karis had drawn as a corridor, the one the seal had confirmed in a paragraph, the one Vastin had been told in two lines and Seln had audited from the other chair for fifteen years — finally said out loud, in a room with a transcript, by the man sent to conduct it, as a courtesy, because he had been asked.
   154	
   155	Karis wrote it down. She did not need to; the clerk had it; but she wrote it in her own hand in the notebook's front matter, beside the fielding clause and the squad clause and the stratum note and the line she could not place, and she dated it, and she closed the book on it with the expression Cael had learned to read as *finding entered*.
   156	
   157	He asked her about it on the steps, afterward, because he wanted the researcher's reading and not his own.
   158	
   159	"Was that worth the provision?"
   160	
   161	"It was worth four sessions and a hundred and forty years," Karis said. "Whether it's worth anything else depends entirely on who reads the minute in ten years' time, and I've stopped pretending I know." She put the notebook away. "He didn't have to say it. That's the part I'll be sitting with. He could have said 'the petitioner declines to speculate' and been correct and been finished. He answered because he was asked and because he thought the answer was obvious." She went down two steps. "It *is* obvious. To him. He's been in this machine for thirty years and it has never once occurred to him that the sentence he just said is the most frightening one on the continent."
   162	
   163	---
   164	
   165	The venue rail, the confluence, the last evening of the meet, the hall emptying below.
   166	
   167	Lira had the bout sheet in her hand — Rooke's four words at its foot, the Rank 8's flags, the three-to-one that was the best loss of her life. Cael had the served demand in his, the production order, the paper an officer had walked a hall to deliver.
   168	
   169	She looked at both papers, one in each of their hands, for a while.
   170	
   171	"One of these is the best night of my life," she said.
   172	
   173	"Noted," Cael said.
   174	
   175	Neither of them said the other half. The hall went on emptying. The river went on outside, wide and slow and the color of weak tea, past a town where a regional Iron title had been decided and a Silver bout had been fought and a registry officer had walked a gallery's length to serve paper at the right hour.
   176	
   177	She folded the bout sheet. Carefully. Along its creases, squared, with more attention than the folding needed — the care she gave to things she intended to keep, and the care, Cael charted without being able to help it, of a woman who was already, somewhere below decision, beginning to say goodbye to a page she had waited three years to be handed.

