   159	"He wasn't mine."
   160	
   161	"No. Walked down my hall, though, didn't he." He shrugged. "This town pays more to the registry than any town on the river. Barge dues, licences, the lot. Nobody here loves it. Nobody here cheers against it either. That's all you heard yesterday, when it went quiet. Not a town that's turned on you. A town remembering what its licences cost." He put his hands back behind him. "They'll come round. We always do, after."
